a Colorado paramedic who died from the  corona virus after volunteering to help  save lives in New York is being  remembered as a hero emotional tributes  were held yesterday as the body of 66  year old Paul Carey was transported home  he had already signed up for another 30  days of service in New York before he  got sick  Jamie yuccas has his story a somber  homecoming Sunday for a man who spent  his life serving others after 30 years  as a firefighter in Aurora Colorado  Paul Carey became a paramedic he didn't  hesitate when asked to join the battle  against coronavirus in New York City  fellow paramedic Royce Davis was he the  type of guy that wanted to run into the  burning building you would not be able  to hold him back that's why Carey came  to New York on April 1st part of a wave  of out-of-state medical staff to assist  the city's overwhelm first responders  often transporting kovat patients from  hospital to hospital less than two weeks  ago he himself became ill before  succumbing to coronavirus last Thursday  as he began his final journey home  New Yorkers lined the streets to pay  tribute powerful respect Mayor Bill de  Blasio says Carey will be among those  honored in a new memorial to medical  workers from around the country who  answered New York's call for help  in a statement Kerry's two sons and four  grandsons say they're at peace knowing  he did what he loved and believed in  right up until the very end what did he  stand for just be humble go help people  doesn't matter what age you are it will  help him for CBS this morning I'm Jamie  yuccas yeah our thanks to Paul Carey for  everything he did for the city in the  state and our condolences to his family
 

